{"name":"Death Cap","hex":"#e7d9db","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"death cap","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The deadly poisonous mushroom Amanita phalloides."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A potentially dangerous capacitor on the primary side of the power transformer in a guitar amp."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/death_cap"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amanita phalloides","blocks":[{"text":"Amanita phalloides, commonly known as the death cap, is a deadly poisonous basidiomycete fungus and mushroom, one of many in the genus Amanita. Originating in Europe but later introduced to other parts of the world since the late twentieth century, A. phalloides forms ectomycorrhizae with various broadleaved trees. In some cases, the death cap has been introduced to new regions with the cultivation of non-native species of oak, chestnut, and pine. The large fruiting bodies appear in summer and autumn; the caps are generally greenish in colour with a white stipe and gills. The cap colour is variable, including white forms, and is thus not a reliable identifier."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Amanita_phalloides_1.JPG/330px-Amanita_phalloides_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amanita_phalloides"},{"title":"Death","blocks":[{"text":"Death is the end of an organism's life. It is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Entropy, nature's tendency toward disorder, can contribute to the breakdown of living systems over time. Upon death, organisms stop exhibiting behavior and generally begin to decompose through various physical and chemical processes. Death can occur at any stage of development, including in infancy. A person may experience clinical death that is reversible, resulting in some cases in a near-death experience. In humans, establishing death of the brain determines that an individual has irreversibly died."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg/330px-Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death"},{"title":"Cap","blocks":[{"text":"A cap is a flat headgear. They made their first appearance as early as 3200 BC. The origin of the word \"cap\" comes from the Old French word \"chapeau\" which means \"head covering\". Over time, the word has evolved and changed its meaning, but it still retains its association with headwear. They are popular in casual and informal settings, and are seen in sports and fashion. They are typically designed for warmth, and often incorporate a visor to block sunlight from the eyes. They come in many shapes and sizes and are of different brands. Baseball caps are one of the most common types of cap."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cap"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Alpha-amanitin_structure.png/500px-Alpha-amanitin_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Alpha-amanitin_structure.png/1280px-Alpha-amanitin_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Alpha-amanitin_structure.png/500px-Alpha-amanitin_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"α-Amanitin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlpha-amanitin_structure.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Photo_of_three_Amanita_phalloides_%28Death_Cap%29.jpg/250px-Photo_of_three_Amanita_phalloides_%28Death_Cap%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Photo_of_three_Amanita_phalloides_%28Death_Cap%29.jpg/500px-Photo_of_three_Amanita_phalloides_%28Death_Cap%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Photo_of_three_Amanita_phalloides_%28Death_Cap%29.jpg/250px-Photo_of_three_Amanita_phalloides_%28Death_Cap%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhoto_of_three_Amanita_phalloides_(Death_Cap).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Amanita_phallo%C3%AFdes.jpg/250px-Amanita_phallo%C3%AFdes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Amanita_phallo%C3%AFdes.jpg/500px-Amanita_phallo%C3%AFdes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Amanita_phallo%C3%AFdes.jpg/250px-Amanita_phallo%C3%AFdes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmanita_phallo%C3%AFdes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Amanita_phalloides_young.jpg/500px-Amanita_phalloides_young.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Amanita_phalloides_young.jpg/960px-Amanita_phalloides_young.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Amanita_phalloides_young.jpg/500px-Amanita_phalloides_young.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmanita_phal